#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163273 :tutorial/content "There are several important properties of the variance of a random variable that we can use to simplify expressions. \n\nFirstly, the variance of a constant is always zero. That is, for any constant $a,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[a] = 0.\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this makes sense. A constant does not vary, so the mean deviation from its expected value should be zero." :tutorial/id #uuid "6eb17ec7-1e57-5d75-8a71-9030a420602b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5458 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163274 :tutorial/content "Suppose that $X$ is a random variable and $a$ is a constant. Then,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[aX] = a^2\\textrm{Var}[X].\n$$\n\nSo, for example, if we know that $\\textrm{Var}[X] = 2,$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[3X] & ={3}^{2}\\cdot \\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & =9\\cdot \\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & =9\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[-4X] & =(-4)^{2}\\cdot \\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & =16\\cdot \\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & =16\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =32.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** To understand why we have to square the constant when we factor it outside the variance, it's helpful to remember that the variance is defined as the expected *squared* deviation from the mean:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X] = \\textrm E [ (X - \\textrm E[X])^2 ]\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[aX] & =\\text{E}[(aX-\\text{E}[aX])^{2}] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[(aX-a\\text{E}[X])^{2}] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{a}^{2}(X-\\text{E}[X])^{2}] \\\\  & ={a}^{2}\\text{E}[(X-\\text{E}[X])^{2}] \\\\  & ={a}^{2}\\text{Var}[X].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "d33b6982-066e-5c82-8138-0283ad8dccbb" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5459 :tutorial/title "The Variance of a Scaled Random Variable"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163275 :tutorial/content "Suppose that $X$ is a random variable and $a$ and $b$ are constants. It can be shown that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[aX+b] = a^2\\textrm{Var}[X].\n$$\n\nA proof of this result is given at the end of this lesson.\n\nTherefore, when a random variable is scaled by a factor of $a$ and shifted by a constant $b\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n- the variance is scaled by a factor of $a^2$ as before, and\n\n- the variance is unaffected by the shift.\n\nThe fact that shifting a random variable leaves the variance unaltered makes intuitive sense. Shifting all values of $X$ by a fixed amount does not change how far apart those values are from each other. \n\nFor example, if we know that $\\textrm{Var}[X] = 3,$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[2X+1] & ={2}^{2}\\cdot \\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & ={2}^{2}\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =4\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "1523e51b-84da-5100-809f-09fbcb0d80ba" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5460 :tutorial/title "The Variance of a Transformed Random Variable"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163276 :tutorial/content "We wish to prove the following result:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[aX+b] = a^2\\textrm{Var}[X]\n$$\n\nRecall that, by definition,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X] = \\textrm E [ (X - \\textrm E[X])^2 ].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[aX+b] & =\\text{E}[(aX+b-\\text{E}[aX+b])^{2}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the properties of expected value, we can simplify this as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[(aX+b-\\text{E}[aX+b])^{2}] & =\\text{E}[(aX+b-a\\text{E}[X]-b)^{2}] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[(aX-a\\text{E}[X])^{2}] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[(a(X-\\text{E}[X]))^{2}] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{a}^{2}(X-\\text{E}[X])^{2}] \\\\  & ={a}^{2}\\text{E}[(X-\\text{E}[X])^{2}] \\\\  & ={a}^{2}\\text{Var}[X]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "0372f0de-b6a6-58ee-8195-c04024e8f745" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5461 :tutorial/title "Proof of the General Formula"}]}
